\section{BearSSL in Ulix: what the library needs from the system}
\label{src:tls:bearssl}

BearSSL is compiled from [[third_party/bearssl/src]] without changes: all 277 files, at optimisation level 2 (the cryptography is
slow at level 0), into an archive; the linker takes only the functions that [[https]] uses. The rules are in
[[userland/Makefile]]. What the library expects of the system is very little:

\begin{itemize}
\item the header [[string.h]] and the five functions [[memcpy]], [[memmove]], [[memset]], [[memcmp]] and [[strlen]]. The user library
      of Ulix ([[ulixlib.c]]) has three of them; the file below adds [[memmove]] (copying that is safe when source and target
      overlap) and [[memcmp]]. The header is our own, three lines, in [[userland/tls/]], and is found before any other because of
      the order of the [[-I]] options;
\item [[-DBR_USE_UNIX_TIME=0]]: the library does not call [[time()]]. The program tells it the time (see [[https]]);
\item nothing else: no [[malloc]], no files, no sockets (the program passes two functions that move bytes), no random numbers (the
      program hands it a seed).
\end{itemize}

<<userland/tls/string.h>>=
#ifndef TLS_STRING_H
#define TLS_STRING_H
#include <stddef.h>
void *memcpy (void *dest, const void *src, size_t n);
void *memmove (void *dest, const void *src, size_t n);
void *memset (void *dest, int c, size_t n);
int   memcmp (const void *a, const void *b, size_t n);
size_t strlen (const char *s);
#endif

@
[[memmove]] copies from the front when the target is below the source, and from the back otherwise. [[memcmp]] stops at the
first byte that differs. (BearSSL compares secrets with its own constant-time functions; these two are for everything else.)

<<userland/tls/libc.c>>=
#include <stddef.h>

void *memmove (void *dest, const void *src, size_t n) {
  unsigned char *d = dest;
  const unsigned char *s = src;
  if (d < s) { for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) d[i] = s[i]; }
  else       { for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) d[i - 1] = s[i - 1]; }
  return dest;
}

int memcmp (const void *a, const void *b, size_t n) {
  const unsigned char *x = a, *y = b;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) if (x[i] != y[i]) return x[i] < y[i] ? -1 : 1;
  return 0;
}

