\section{\texttt{userland/https.c}: fetch a page over TLS}
\label{src:userland:https.c}

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    https [-i] [-v] [-c cafile] [-n name] [-t timeserver] https://host[:port]/path
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

The program is a small [[curl]]: it writes the body of the answer to the screen ([[-i]]: with the header, [[-v]]: it tells what
was agreed). What it does, step by step, each one with its own message when it fails:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Is TLS switched on?} [[tls_enable]] in [[/etc/rc.conf]] (default YES). If not: ``TLS is disabled'', exit status 1, nothing
      else happens (no connection, no look at the file system).
\item \emph{Random numbers.} 32 bytes from [[getrandom]] seed BearSSL's own generator (HMAC\_DRBG). Without a source the program
      stops.
\item \emph{The time.} The certificate has dates. If the clock of the system is a guess (the Raspberry Pi after the boot), the
      program asks a time server ([[-t]], default [[pool.ntp.org]]) and uses that time for the checks. A program of an ordinary
      user must not set the clock; [[sntp -s]] does that for root.
\item \emph{The certificate authorities} from [[/etc/ssl/cacert.pem]] (or the file of [[-c]]): the file is read, the PEM text is turned
      into certificates one at a time, and each one that is a CA is kept as a ``trust anchor'': its name and its public key.
\item \emph{Name and connection.} The name is resolved ([[resolve_host]]) and the TCP connection is opened ([[tcp_connect]]).
\item \emph{The handshake.} BearSSL does it while the program sends the request: it checks that the chain of certificates leads to one
      of the anchors, that every certificate is valid today, that the server's certificate is for the host name (the name
      in the URL, or the one of [[-n]]), and that the server owns the key. A failure ends the connection before any byte of the
      request has left.
\item \emph{HTTP.} A request in version 1.0 ([[GET /path HTTP/1.0]] with a [[Host:]] line): the server answers without chunks and closes the
      connection at the end, so the end of the data is the end of the page.
\end{enumerate}

The option [[-n name]] means ``use this name for the Host line and for the certificate check, whatever the URL says''. The
tests need it: the test server is reached as [[10.0.2.2]] (the host of qemu's network), but its certificate is for a name.

The program is split in two files because the two sides do not understand each other's header files: BearSSL uses the standard
types of [[<stdint.h>]], the user library of Ulix declares its own ([[uint64_t]] is a different type in each). So everything that
touches BearSSL is in [[userland/tls/tlsglue.c]], which knows nothing of Ulix and offers eight small functions with plain types;
[[https.c]] knows Ulix (system calls, [[printf]], arguments) and nothing of BearSSL.

<<userland/https.c>>=
<<userland/https.c: includes>>

<<userland/https.c: glue>>

<<userland/https.c: main>>
@

<<userland/https.c: includes>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"
#include "../arm64-extra.h"

#define DEFAULT_CA_FILE   "/etc/ssl/cacert.pem"
#define DEFAULT_TIME_SERVER "pool.ntp.org"

// the eight functions of tls/tlsglue.c
int  tls_load_anchors (int (*read_bytes) (void *buf, int n));      // the certificate authorities from PEM text: how many, or -1
int  tls_start (const char *name, unsigned unix_seconds, const unsigned char *seed, int (*in) (unsigned char *, int), int (*out) (const unsigned char *, int));
int  tls_write (const void *buf, int len);                         // send, and wait until it has been sent; < 0: error
int  tls_read (void *buf, int len);                                // > 0: data, <= 0: end or error
int  tls_error (void);                                             // 0 = no error
const char *tls_error_text (int error);
void tls_session (int *version, int *cipher_suite);
void tls_close (void);

static int sock = -1;                                              // the TCP socket of the connection
static int ca_fd = -1;                                             // the file of the certificate authorities

@
BearSSL asks for bytes through two functions, and these call the system calls of the TCP sockets. [[low_read]] waits for the first
byte (at most 30 seconds); [[tcp_recv]] returns 0 when the time is over and $-1$ when the other side has closed. The library treats
both as ``the connection is broken'', which is what they are, unless the data was complete (see the end of [[main]]).

<<userland/https.c: glue>>=
static int low_read (unsigned char *buf, int len) {
  struct tcp_msg m;
  m.handle = sock;  m.buf = buf;  m.len = len > 4096 ? 4096 : len;  m.timeout_ms = 30000;
  int n = tcp_recv (&m);
  return n > 0 ? n : -1;
}

static int low_write (const unsigned char *buf, int len) {
  struct tcp_msg m;
  m.handle = sock;  m.buf = (unsigned char *)buf;  m.len = len;  m.timeout_ms = 30000;
  int n = tcp_send (&m);
  return n > 0 ? n : -1;
}

static int read_ca_file (void *buf, int n) { return read (ca_fd, buf, n); }

